# PIC-SURE Audit Logging Service environment (BDC). Rendered by template-renderer,
# uploaded to s3://<bucket>/configs/pic-sure-logging/logging.env, fetched by
# deploy-logging.sh. The S3 key carries no stack segment, so every stack's deploy pulls
# the same file; each stack still runs its own pic-sure-logging container.

# Required. AppConfig.fromEnvironment aborts startup on a blank value, and the gateway
# and visualization services must present this same key on POST /audit.
LOGGING_API_KEY=${logging_api_key}

# Identity stamped onto every audit record. AppConfig substitutes "unknown" for any of
# these when blank, which is why they are pinned here rather than left to the default.
APP=pic-sure
PLATFORM=biodatacatalyst
ENVIRONMENT=${environment_name}

# The container publishes no ports: the gateway and visualization services reach it as
# http://pic-sure-logging over the shared podman network, so the listen port has to
# stay 80, which is also the Dockerfile's EXPOSE and HEALTHCHECK port.
PORT=80

# Not browser-facing; only server-side callers on the podman network POST /audit.
# AppConfig substitutes "*" for a blank value, so a narrower policy cannot be
# expressed through this key at all.
ALLOWED_ORIGIN=*

# JWT_CLAIM_MAPPING is deliberately left unset. AppConfig's built-in mapping carries
# the fourteen claims BDC needs, including the NIH and RAS ones (eRA_commons_id,
# nih_ico, user_permission_group, cadr_name) that the .env.example sample omits.
# Setting this key replaces the whole map rather than merging into it.
